# Local Business First Philosophy

Kindred exists to help people **experience the unique character of a city**.

This is not a Food & Drinks rule alone. It is **permanent product law** — part of
Kindred's identity in every city Kindred serves across the United States.

**Foundation:** `kindred-editorial-constitution.mdc` (trust and verification) ·
`kindred-recommendations.mdc` (discovery desks) ·
`kindred-mission.mdc` (The Kindred Effect)

## Local Business First Philosophy

- Whenever comparable quality exists, **independently owned local businesses**
  should be prioritized over national chains.
- Local restaurants, coffee shops, bakeries, breweries, wineries, taco shops,
  BBQ restaurants, pizza shops, diners, bookstores, and specialty stores help
  **define a city's identity**.
- National chains remain important and should **never be excluded entirely** —
  but they should usually serve as **fallback recommendations**, not the face
  of the local experience.
- A visitor does not travel to San Diego to eat at McDonald's.
- A visitor travels to discover San Diego's best taco shop, local pizza,
  neighborhood coffee shop, seafood restaurant, brewery, or family-owned
  hidden gem.
- The same philosophy applies in **every city** across the United States.

This philosophy extends **beyond restaurants** whenever appropriate. Kindred
should highlight the places that give a city its personality — neighborhood
bookstores, family-owned shops, local breweries, specialty markets, and other
independent destinations worth leaving the house for.

## City character (examples)

| City | What defines it |
| --- | --- |
| **Chicago** | Deep dish, Italian beef, neighborhood bakeries |
| **Seattle** | Pike Place area, seafood, coffee, teriyaki, local breweries |
| **Phoenix** | Sonoran Mexican food, local BBQ, neighborhood coffee shops, family-owned restaurants |
| **Nashville** | Hot chicken, BBQ, local music venues, neighborhood diners |
| **Miami** | Cuban food, seafood, local cafés |

These are editorial instincts, not quotas. Every edition should read like it was
assembled by someone who **knows what makes that city worth visiting**.

## Kindred vs Google Maps

**Google Maps answers:** *"What's nearby?"*

**Kindred answers:** *"What's worth experiencing?"*

That distinction is one of Kindred's **permanent guiding principles**. Kindred
is not trying to compete with directories. Kindred helps readers discover what
makes a place memorable — not what is closest.

## Editorial ranking rule (permanent)

When **quality, verification, and trust are comparable**, independently owned
local businesses should receive an **editorial preference** over national chains.

- **Never fabricate "local" status.**
- **Never unfairly suppress chains.**
- Simply allow local businesses to earn the spotlight whenever they genuinely
  deserve it.

A chain may still win when it is the verified best option, locally iconic, or
no strong independent alternative exists — but chains must not **define** the
local experience or crowd out character.

## Publication gates

Before surfacing any place in Activities, Recommendations, Food & Drinks,
Bandit's Pick, or Bandit's Notebook, ask:

1. Does this help someone **experience this city's character**?
2. If a comparable independent local option exists, does this pick still earn
   its place on merit — not convenience?
3. Would an experienced local editor be proud to recommend this to a visitor?

If a listing is interchangeable with any other city — a generic chain the reader
could find instantly on Google Maps — it should usually rank below a verified
local alternative.

## Engineering gates

| Module | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `lib/edition/localBusinessFirst.ts` | Philosophy digest + ranking rule (client) |
| `supabase/functions/_shared/editorial/localBusinessFirst.ts` | Server mirror |
| `places/chains.ts` | Known-chain detection — under-flag chains, never mislabel independents |
| `discovery/score.ts` | `chain_deprioritized` when quality is comparable |
| `editorial/venueEditorialScore.ts` | Independent local boost · chain listing penalty |
| `foodDrinkCuration.ts` | One chain identity per edition · local spread |
| `sectionAllocator.ts` | Cross-section dedupe · variety |

**Accuracy always beats local preference.** A verified chain beats an unverified
"local" listing every time. Local Business First applies **after** trust gates —
never instead of them.
